#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6ae25fc-16eb-598b-a288-742e4b46cb12" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd18a542-5ea7-5e67-9fa4-c5b47b4395b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd18a542-5ea7-5e67-9fa4-c5b47b4395b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362173} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2bc28ef-3d08-5a5d-862c-366274de3c47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f22cc924-1da3-5523-8968-0ab0dbbca38c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f22cc924-1da3-5523-8968-0ab0dbbca38c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362175} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ab1c19-9740-5a14-8666-f31e9ec74572" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa9d930a-9bdd-55b4-a90a-3a636e8591e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-139406" :question/problem "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to extend the game and make it so that the winner of each round receives $$2$ from the loser. So, for example, if Maria picks scissors and John picks rock, then Maria pays $$2$ to John.\n\nMaria represents the money she could earn or lose after one game using the following table.\n\n|  |  | John |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rock** | **Paper** | **Scissors** |\n| Maria | **Rock** | $0$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| **Paper** | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  |\n| **Scissors** | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nCurrent part:\n\nMaria represents the information in the table using the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The dimensions of $P$ are$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\times $\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $R$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cc70bda-40cf-5860-9a47-25f290c71041" :knowledge-point/title "Describing an Entry of a Matrix in Index Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9cab97-d0ff-5ca1-abb4-bf695dbe9544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f9cab97-d0ff-5ca1-abb4-bf695dbe9544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362178} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5a6f1a-3f92-5519-95a6-3146a599be24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362177} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f985e32-78eb-53db-a254-5871900b145f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f985e32-78eb-53db-a254-5871900b145f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362180} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e429f22-963e-5871-8a12-7f3b066ab5d1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a0e57a6-5579-559d-9cc8-159f6d31c49d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139426" :question/problem "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to extend the game and make it so that the winner of each round receives $$2$ from the loser. So, for example, if Maria picks scissors and John picks rock, then Maria pays $$2$ to John.\n\nMaria represents the money she could earn or lose after one game using the following table.\n\n|  |  | John |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rock** | **Paper** | **Scissors** |\n| Maria | **Rock** | $0$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| **Paper** | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  |\n| **Scissors** | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nMaria represents the information in the table using the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The dimensions of $P$ are$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\times $\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn index notation, the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ above is ${p}_{ij},$ where$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $i=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         and \n$j=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "The entry ${p}_{ij}$ lies at the intersection of the $3$rd row and $2$nd column. Hence, we have $i=3$ and $j=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2da3580a-6a1a-5e38-bcab-bd35f1ea5a6e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Scalar"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19849b0b-64aa-5701-b11a-655cfcf57afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19849b0b-64aa-5701-b11a-655cfcf57afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362183} :answer-field/id #uuid "2186aacf-80a5-584b-bb30-3226dce3e3a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4aee6b82-1f19-5bf9-9820-c5feebad97b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-139431" :question/problem "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to extend the game and make it so that the winner of each round receives $$2$ from the loser. So, for example, if Maria picks scissors and John picks rock, then Maria pays $$2$ to John.\n\nMaria represents the money she could earn or lose after one game using the following table.\n\n|  |  | John |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rock** | **Paper** | **Scissors** |\n| Maria | **Rock** | $0$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| **Paper** | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  |\n| **Scissors** | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nMaria represents the information in the table using the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The dimensions of $P$ are$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\times $\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn index notation, the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ above is ${p}_{ij},$ where$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $i=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         and \n$j=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nMaria and John agreed to increase the bet. After increasing, Maria's payoff matrix became\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis new matrix is obtained from matrix $P$ using the scalar multiplication $kP$, where\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,k=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "To express $kP,$ we multiply each entry of $P$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kP & =k[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2k & 2k \\\\ 2k & 0 & -2k \\\\ -2k & 2k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the value given for $kP$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2k & 2k \\\\ 2k & 0 & -2k \\\\ -2k & 2k & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the top-right elements, $2k$ and $6.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would get the same result by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53675175-8261-54ef-8dfb-2b3087b91239" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Probability Mass Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6bd02b2-3ecd-556c-9ab1-e6fc39f05e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bd02b2-3ecd-556c-9ab1-e6fc39f05e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362186} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62bd0f8-894f-5913-9519-693af2ac6f68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362185} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7ce4373-49f9-56b2-90ba-9bcab5e2333e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7ce4373-49f9-56b2-90ba-9bcab5e2333e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362188} :answer-field/id #uuid "139d5838-7709-5d57-86d5-11e2448d325e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4a6415e-8605-5b6c-a361-58c8f9d5c24e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139440" :question/problem "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to extend the game and make it so that the winner of each round receives $$2$ from the loser. So, for example, if Maria picks scissors and John picks rock, then Maria pays $$2$ to John.\n\nMaria represents the money she could earn or lose after one game using the following table.\n\n|  |  | John |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rock** | **Paper** | **Scissors** |\n| Maria | **Rock** | $0$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| **Paper** | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  |\n| **Scissors** | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nMaria represents the information in the table using the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The dimensions of $P$ are$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\times $\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn index notation, the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ above is ${p}_{ij},$ where$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $i=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         and \n$j=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nMaria and John agreed to increase the bet. After increasing, Maria's payoff matrix became\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis new matrix is obtained from matrix $P$ using the scalar multiplication $kP$, where\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,k=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThey then agreed that both would put two dollars into a saving box in case of a draw. Hence, the payoff matrix for Maria is now \n\n$$\nP-2{I}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSuppose that each outcome in the game is equally likely. Let $X$ be the profit Maria can earn after playing the game once with the payoff matrix above. The probability mass function for $X$ is given below.\n\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $q$ | $r$ |\n\nFind the values of $q$ and $r.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $q=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,r=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "In each instance of the game, there are three possible outcomes for $X$:\n\n* The result $X=-6$ occurs $3$ times out of $9$ in the payoff matrix. Therefore the corresponding probability is $\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n* The result $X=-2$ occurs $3$ times out of $9$ in the payoff matrix. Therefore the corresponding probability is $\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n* The result $X=6$ occurs $3$ times out of $9$ in the payoff matrix. Therefore the corresponding probability is $\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nHence, the probability mass function for $X$ is\n\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nHence, $q=r=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35193ec5-030d-5c78-b536-08e23bd3b7d4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within a Set of Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400e6144-8fe4-5de8-a4ee-5fb9428ac2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "400e6144-8fe4-5de8-a4ee-5fb9428ac2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186